The processed Lakehouse data provides the source for the business transformations. Individual transformation logic is then applied to create the required Dimension and Fact datasets.

10.3 Dimension Tables

The project contains 15 Dimension tables implemented in Microsoft Fabric.

Dimension tables contain descriptive and reference information required for reporting and analytical processing. They provide business context that can be associated with the transactional or measurable information maintained in the Fact tables.

Each Dimension table is created using its own transformation code based on the corresponding SQL implementation.

Depending on the business requirement, Dimension processing may include:

Reading the required source datasets

Joining related source tables

Applying business filters

Selecting required attributes

Renaming and mapping columns

Applying business rules

Creating derived attributes

Handling null/default values

Removing duplicates where required

Applying data type conversions

Preparing required keys and reference values

Loading the final Dimension table into Fabric

The transformation logic differs by Dimension table based on its business purpose and source data.

10.4 Fact Tables

The project contains 3 Fact tables implemented in Microsoft Fabric.

Fact tables contain the transactional, event-level, or measurable business information required for reporting and analytics.

Fact processing generally combines the required source data with related Dimension information and applies the necessary business rules before loading the final Fact table.

Depending on the table requirement, Fact processing may include:

Reading multiple source datasets

Joining related datasets

Referencing Dimension tables where required

Applying business filters and conditions

Mapping required keys

Performing calculations

Creating derived measures or attributes

Applying date and timestamp transformations

Handling null/default values

Maintaining the required record grain

Selecting the final target columns

Loading the completed Fact table into Fabric

Each Fact table has its own implementation logic based on the corresponding existing SQL process and business requirements.

10.5 Transformation and Loading Process

Although each Dimension and Fact table contains different business logic, the overall transformation and loading approach follows a common pattern.

Source Data Selection

The required source datasets are identified based on the existing SQL implementation and target table requirements.

The processing reads the required data from the appropriate Lakehouse layers and selects the columns necessary to construct the target Dimension or Fact table.

Data Transformation

Once the source data is identified, table-specific transformation logic is applied.

The transformations may include:

Source-to-source joins

Filtering conditions

Column selection and mapping

Conditional/CASE logic

Data type conversions

Derived columns

Business calculations

Date transformations

Null/default value handling

Duplicate handling

Key mapping

Business-specific rules

The transformation logic in Microsoft Fabric is designed to maintain the same required business outcome as the corresponding SQL implementation.

Dimension Transformation

For Dimension tables, the processing primarily prepares the descriptive and reference attributes required for analytical use.

The source data is transformed into the required Dimension structure, and the necessary business attributes and keys are prepared before loading.

Fact Transformation

For Fact tables, the processing prepares the required transactional or measurable information.

Source datasets are joined according to the defined business relationships, Dimension information is incorporated where required, and the necessary calculations and measures are prepared.

The required Fact grain is maintained so that each record represents the intended business-level information.

Target Mapping

After the business transformations are completed, the resulting dataset is mapped to the corresponding Fabric target structure.

The mapping ensures consistency in:

Column names

Data types

Required keys

Target attributes

Mandatory values

Data Loading

The transformed dataset is then loaded into the corresponding Dimension or Fact table in Microsoft Fabric.

Each table uses its own implementation code to perform the required transformations and final target load.

The overall process is:

Source Data → Joins & Filters → Business Rules → Transformations → Target Mapping → DIM/FACT Load

